  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\n___\n\nChief Complaint:\nLeg stiffness\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n___ with PMH of uterine polyps s/p TAH/BSO, mitral valve\nprolapse, hypertension who presented to the ED with L leg\nstiffness. She was referred by her son, Dr ___, who is a\nneurologist here at ___.\n\nPatient states that she was in her usual state of health until\nlast ___, when she went to ___ class. Prior to that she\nhad not gone to ___ class for a long time, but she stays \nactive\nby walking 2 miles a day. After her ___ class, she felt like\nher L leg was stiff. She denies pain or shooting sensation down\nthe leg; it just felt stiff and made it difficult for her to\nwalk. She still walks independently without use of walker or\ncane; however, she feels that she now sometimes needs to hold\nonto objects to keep herself steady. She also endorses bilateral\ntingling sensation in both feet which she has had for at least a\nyear. No tingling in hands.\n\nPatient denies lightheadedness, vision changes, nausea, \nvomiting,\ndiarrhea, fever, incontinence, dysuria, or recent weight \nchanges.\nShe states her appetite is good. However, her husband passed \naway\n6 weeks ago and she is still adjusting to that change. She now\nlives alone and is able to manage her own affairs independently.\nShe states she 'sometimes' has hypertension. Her PCP prescribed\nher nifedipine, but she stopped taking it because it made her\nfoot uncomfortable ?swelling.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n___ is an ___ yo woman w/ pmhx of HTN who presents for \nevaluation of left leg discomfort/stiffness and ambulation \ndifficulties. \n\nTransitional Issues \n==================== \n[ ] Patient has concerns about nifedipine re:Edema, however only \ntook one dose of this medication and has not been taking it \nsince. She was hypertensive this admission w/ SBPs ___ \n[ ] Patient given prescription for outpatient physical therapy\n[ ] A1c 5.8 \n\n#Leg Stiffness \n#Difficulty Ambulating \nOverall patient presenting w/ left leg stiffness and a more \ncautious gait. Her neurologic exam was largely normal with \nexception of perhaps a very subtle hyperreflexia (R>L) that is \nlikely not clinically significant. Her gait did appear a bit \nantalgic, however she had full strength in all isolated muscle \ngroups. She was not having active pain at the time of this \nevaluation. No history of recent falls or trauma. Ultimately we \nattribute this largely to an overuse injury in the setting of \nrecent ___ classes (in addition to her normal 2+ miles of \nwalking daily). She will likely benefit from further physical \ntherapy which we have provided her a prescription for. \n\n#Foot Numbness\nPt does note about a one year history of intermittent foot \nnumbness and feeling of \"rubber on the bottom of her feet\", \nwhich by history may be compatible with the beginning of a small \nfiber neuropathy. On examination she only has a slight decrease \nin position sense of her right big toe, with an otherwise normal \nsensory exam, possibly consistent with an early small fiber \nneuropathy. TSH, B12, and electrolyte were within normal limits. \nA1c slightly elevated at 5.8 in the ___ range. \n\n#HTN \nPt noted to be hypertensive overnight w/ SBP ___. Recently \nstarted on nifedipine as an outpatient, but only took 1 dose \nbefore stopping (she had a leg cramp and was worried about \nedema).We did not start any new medications while she was \ninpatient.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nMusculoskeletal Pain",
